+// The generic family a family that is not on the device is measured as.
+//
+// A family that is not installed does not measure as nothing: the
+// browser substitutes a font for it, and the substitution for a missing
+// family is the generic family of the kind of text.  So the width of a
+// missing family is the width of the generic font, and that is the
+// width the standard font probe compares against.
+//
+// The generic family that stands in for a missing family is the
+// *sans-serif* one.  The probe compares a family's width against the
+// widths of monospace, sans-serif and serif, so a missing family has
+// to measure as one of those three -- and the sans-serif one is the
+// one a device without the family would use.
+//
+// The name comes from the platform's own settings, so the substituted
+// font is the one the platform would really use.  That matters: a
+// family that measures as a font the platform does not have is as
+// suspicious as one that measures as a font the platform does.
+AtomicString GenericFamilyForFallback(
+    const GenericFontFamilySettings& settings,
+    const FontDescription& font_description) {
+  UScriptCode script = font_description.GetScriptForFont();
+  return settings.Get(GenericFontFamily::kSansSerif, script);
+}
